<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Circular-arc graphs are graphs that can be represented as intersection graphs of subpaths of a cycle. Interval graphs are graphs that can be represented as intersection graphs of subpaths of a path. Since cycles are locally paths, every circular-arc graph is locally interval. In this paper, we prove that the converse holds as well: every locally interval graph is a circular-arc graph. This result and its proofs are connected to a recent broader study of structural local-global theory and build on previous work on locally chordal graphs.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Locally interval, interval, chordal, local covering
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If every vertex in a connected graph has degree at most two, then the graph is a path or a cycle. This observation can be extended: if the radius-<math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> ball around every vertex of a connected graph <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a path, then the graph <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is either a path or a cycle of length greater than <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r</annotation></semantics></math>. Since small-radius balls in a long cycle are all paths, this is a characterization: a graph is a path or a long cycle if and only if every small-radius ball of the graph is a path.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we take this simple idea — that paths and long cycles are precisely the shapes that are locally paths everywhere — and apply it to a richer class of graphs. Roughly, we prove that the graphs that are locally “path-like” everywhere are precisely the graphs that are globally “path-like” or “long cycle-like.” To make this precise, let us give some definitions. A graph <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">interval graph</em> if there is a path <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and a mapping <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> from vertices <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to subpaths <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that there is an edge <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> intersect in at least one vertex. We call such a mapping <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> an <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">interval representation (of <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>)</em>. Interval graphs are a special case of more general graphs, region intersection graphs. A graph <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">region intersection graph over <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math></em> if there is a mapping <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> from vertices <math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to connected subgraphs (i.e. <em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">regions</em>) <math id="S1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge of <math id="S1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> intersect in at least one vertex. The mapping <math id="S1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <em id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">region representation (of <math id="S1.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>)</em>. In this language, interval graphs are the class of graphs which admit region representations over paths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Because of their characterization as precisely the graphs which admit region representations over paths, interval graphs can be reasonably understood as graphs which are “path-like.” We are interested in graphs which are “locally path-like,” so let us next understand what we mean by “locally.” Given a graph <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and a vertex <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">ball of radius <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math></em>, denoted <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(v,r/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">B</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">v</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">2</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(v,r/2)</annotation></semantics></math></em>, is the subgraph of <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with vertex set consisting of the vertices of distance at most <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor r/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor r/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and edge set consisting of the edges <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(v,x)+d(v,y)&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(v,x)+d(v,y)&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>. A graph <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval</em> for an integer <math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math> if all its <math id="S1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-balls <math id="S1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(v,r/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(v,r/2)</annotation></semantics></math> are interval graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">What kind of graphs might be locally interval? Since every interval graph has a region representation over a path, it seems sensible to look for graphs that have a region representation over a graph that is “locally a path.” Therefore, a good guess seems to be that locally interval graphs are exactly those graphs which have region representations over a (long) cycle. These form a well-known graph class, the <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">circular-arc</em> graphs. A graph <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">circular-arc</em> if it has a region representation <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> over a cycle <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> such that the regions <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are paths.
We refer to such region representations over cycles as <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">circular-arc representations</em>. A circular-arc representation <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of a graph <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> over a cycle <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic</em> if for every vertex-set <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, the subgraph <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{x\in X}C_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{x\in X}C_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. We call a graph <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> an <em id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic</em> circular-arc graph if <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="S1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc representation.
Note that <math id="S1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graphs are precisely interval graphs, because <math id="S1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{v\in V(G)}C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{v\in V(G)}C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is then a path.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With these definitions, the following intuitive statement, conjectured by Paweł Rzążewski in private communication, seems morally true:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Rzążewski 2024)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Locally interval graphs are exactly circular-arc graphs.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We make this conjecture precise via our definitions above. Specifically, we ask: are the <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval graphs precisely the <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graphs? It turns out that this is false for <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>, but we prove that it is true for <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite connected graph and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math> an integer. Then, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval if and only if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Circular-arc graphs are widely studied. Recently, Cao, Grippo, and Safe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> gave a forbidden induced subgraph characterization of 3-acyclic circular-arc graphs; see <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Cao, Grippo &amp; Safe [], Theorem 1). ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Building on their work, we give a forbidden induced subgraph characterization of <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval graphs for <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Connections to local-global theory</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to addressing an intuitive and visually appealing idea, our study of locally interval graphs connects to a broader study of local-global graph theory.
This line of study began with the work of Diestel, Jacobs, Knappe, and Kurkofka <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, which introduced an idea called the <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local covering</em> to study the “<math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local” structure of a graph. Given a graph <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local covering of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math></em>, denoted <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}:G_{r}\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">p</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#008000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}:G_{r}\to G</annotation></semantics></math></em>, is the universal covering of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that preserves its <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-balls. (See <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. The local covering ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> for the formal definition.) The graph <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local cover of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math></em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, the first and fourth authors and Kobler studied <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally chordal graphs</em>. A graph is <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">chordal</em> if each of its cycles of length at least four has a chord. Equivalently, a graph <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is chordal if and only if <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> admits a region representation over a tree. Since interval graphs are the graphs that admit region representations over paths, interval graphs are a subclass of chordal graphs. A graph is <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally chordal</em> if each of its <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-balls is chordal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem characterizes locally chordal graphs by their <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local covers, by forbidden induced subgraphs, and by region representations. Formal definitions for statements (iii) and (iv) below can be found in <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Locally chordal graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Theorem 1 and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Theorem 1)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph and let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer. The following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-locally chordal.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-local cover </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is chordal.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-chordal and wheel-free.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an </span><math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-acyclic region intersection graph.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our main result in this paper is an analogous theorem for locally interval graphs, giving characterizations by their local covers, by forbidden induced subgraphs, and as circular-arc graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmainresult1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainresult">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmainresult1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmmainresult1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmainresult1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmainresult1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainresult1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmmainresult1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite connected graph and <math id="Thmmainresult1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math> an integer.
Then the following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-locally interval.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-local cover </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is interval.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-chordal, wheel-free, and contains none of the graphs depicted in </span><a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as an induced subgraph. If </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> also contains none of the graphs depicted in </span><a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as an induced subgraph.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-acyclic circular-arc.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Locally chordal graphs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by giving the definitions necessary to understand statements (iii) and (iv) of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3 ([], Theorem 1 and [], Theorem 1). ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. A graph is <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-chordal</em> if each of its cycles of length at least four and at most <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> has a chord. Equivalently, a graph is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-chordal if it does not contain an induced cycle of length <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\leqslant\ell\leqslant r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\leqslant\ell\leqslant r</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">wheel <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">W</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></em> for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph consisting of a cycle <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and an additional vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> that is adjacent to every vertex of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. A graph is <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">wheel-free</em> if it does not contain a wheel <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math> as an induced subgraph. Observe that every chordal graph is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-chordal and wheel-free. The equivalence between (i) and (iii) in <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3 ([], Theorem 1 and [], Theorem 1). ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> states that being <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-chordal and wheel-free is equivalent to being <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally chordal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">region</em> of a graph <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected subgraph of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. A family of regions <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> of a graph <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">Helly</em> if it satisfies the <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">Helly property</em>: every finite subset of pairwise intersecting regions of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> has nonempty intersection. A region representation <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of a graph <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> over a graph <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic</em> if for every set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> of size at most <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, the subgraph <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{x\in X}H_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{x\in X}H_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. A graph <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic region intersection graph</em> if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> admits an <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic region representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we give two statements from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> that we will use in the proof of our main result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Lemma 3.6)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of regions of a (possibly infinite) graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is 3-acyclic if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is 2-acyclic and Helly.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Theorem 5)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> be a 3-acyclic region representation of a (possibly infinite) graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> over a (possibly infinite) graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer. Then, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally chordal if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto H_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto H_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Interval graphs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Boland &amp; Lekkeikerker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> characterized finite interval graphs by forbidden induced subgraphs:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Boland &amp; Lekkeikerker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, II)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a (possibly infinite)<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Even though Boland and Lekkeikerker prove </span><a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Boland &amp; Lekkeikerker [], II). ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a><span id="footnote1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> only for finite graphs, Halin </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="footnote1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a><span id="footnote1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">, (8)]</span></cite><span id="footnote1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> showed that an infinite graph is interval if and only if its finite induced subgraphs are interval, which implies </span><a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Boland &amp; Lekkeikerker [], II). ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a><span id="footnote1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> for infinite graphs.</span></span></span></span> graph.
Then the following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is interval.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is chordal and contains none of the graphs depicted in </span><a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as an induced subgraph.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
