</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Chordal minimal forbidden induced subgraphs of interval graphs. The dotted lines in <a href="#S2.F1.sf3" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1c</span></a> and <a href="#S2.F1.sf4" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1d</span></a> represent paths of arbitrary length, except one of the dotted lines in <a href="#S2.F1.sf3" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1c</span></a> must be of length at least one.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a sanity check, we show that every interval graph is indeed locally interval. (Observe that this is not immediate from the definition. Every induced subgraph of an interval graph is an interval graph, but when <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is even, the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-balls of a graph are not necessarily induced subgraphs.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every (possibly infinite) interval graph is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval for every integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be an interval graph and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 0</annotation></semantics></math> an integer.
Since interval graphs are chordal, the graph <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is chordal, and so in particular, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-chordal and wheel-free. Therefore, by <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3 ([], Theorem 1 and [], Theorem 1). ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally chordal.
Suppose for a contradiction that an <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-ball <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> around a vertex <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> contains a graph <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as induced subgraph.
If <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the non-interval graph <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is also an induced subgraph of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, assume that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is even, and set <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\coloneqq r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\coloneqq r/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is not an induced subgraph of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, some two vertices of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> have to lie in <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{d}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{d}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{d}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{d}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of vertices of distance exactly <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3 ([], Theorem 1 and [], Theorem 1). ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, for every vertex <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in N^{d}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in N^{d}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, its neighborhood <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{B}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{B}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is a clique in <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, only the red circled vertices in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> may be contained in <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{d}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{d}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is a simple check that every potential edge of <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> between some pair of red circled vertices yields an induced cycle of length at least <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is chordal.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>The local covering</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we introduce the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local covering. A <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">covering</em> of a connected graph <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjective mapping <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:C\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:C\to G</annotation></semantics></math> from a graph <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that for every vertex <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, the mapping <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to an isomorphism from the edges incident to <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> (in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>) to the edges incident to <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)</annotation></semantics></math> (in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>). Given an integer <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, a covering <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:C\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:C\to G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S2.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-ball-preserving</em> if <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to an isomorphism from <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(c,r/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(c,r/2)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(p(c),r/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(p(c),r/2)</annotation></semantics></math> for every vertex <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. A covering <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:C\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:C\to G</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">universal</em> among a set of coverings <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> if for every covering <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:D\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:D\to G</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a covering <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:C\to D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:C\to D</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=q\circ s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=q\circ s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The formal definition of the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local covering is given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, §4.2]</cite>. For this paper, we need only its equivalent characterization given by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Lemmas 4.2, 4.3, and 4.4]</cite>. Given a connected graph <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local covering <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}:G_{r}\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">p</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#008000" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}:G_{r}\to G</annotation></semantics></math></em> is the universal covering among the coverings of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that preserve <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-balls. The graph <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;"><math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local cover</em> of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 1</annotation></semantics></math>, both the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local covering <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}:G_{r}\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}:G_{r}\to G</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local cover <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> are unique.</p>
</div>
