<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Proof of Theorem 1</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that for <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval graphs are precisely the <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graphs, i.e. <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Conjecture 1.1 (Rzążewski 2024). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> holds for <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Cao, Grippo and Safe characterized the <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graphs by forbidden induced subgraphs (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Cao, Grippo &amp; Safe [], Theorem 1). ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).
They refer to <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graphs as <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normal Helly circular-arc graphs</em>.
In the literature, <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graphs are called <em id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">normal</em>.
These types of circular-arc graphs have often been considered when being additionally <em id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Helly</em>.
By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1 ([], Lemma 3.6). ‣ 2.1. Locally chordal graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graphs are precisely the normal Helly circular-arc graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Cao, Grippo &amp; Safe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, Theorem 1)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite graph. Then the following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-acyclic (equivalently: normal Helly) circular-arc.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is wheel-free (equivalently: </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-locally chordal) and contains none of the graphs depicted in </span><a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figures</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as an induced subgraph.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
