<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Additional forbidden induced subgraphs for <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graphs in the case <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to prove <a href="#Thmmainresult1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#Thmmainresult1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove <a href="#S1.I2.i2" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a><math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math><a href="#S1.I2.i1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(i)</span></a><math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math><a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a><math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math><a href="#S1.I2.i2" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a>, <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a><math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math><a href="#S1.I2.i4" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iv)</span></a>, and <a href="#S1.I2.i4" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iv)</span></a><math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math><a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 1</span></span><span id="Thmclaim1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.I2.i2" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a><span id="Thmclaim1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> implies <a href="#S1.I2.i1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(i)</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S1.I2.i2" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a>, the <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local cover <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is interval, and thus <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> yields that <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval. As <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-balls, it follows that <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 2</span></span><span id="Thmclaim2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmclaim2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every graph <math id="Thmclaim2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, there is a vertex <math id="Thmclaim2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmclaim2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmclaim2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\subseteq B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\subseteq B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For every graph <math id="Thmclaim2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, there is a vertex <math id="Thmclaim2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmclaim2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)</annotation></semantics></math> is the long claw.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">In both cases, let <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be the green vertex in the respective figure. The blue vertices are the ones with distance <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 3</span></span><span id="Thmclaim3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.I2.i1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(i)</span></a><span id="Thmclaim3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> implies <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval, it holds that <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally chordal. Thus, by <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3 ([], Theorem 1 and [], Theorem 1). ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is wheel-free and <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-chordal.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose for a contradiction that <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a graph from <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as an induced subgraph <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)\subseteq B_{G}(v_{F},4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)\subseteq B_{G}(v_{F},4/2)</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#Thmclaim2" title="Claim 2. ‣ Proof of . ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Claim</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Since <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of the interval graph <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(v_{F},r/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(v_{F},r/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a contradiction to <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Boland &amp; Lekkeikerker [], II). ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.
This completes the proof of the claim for <math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now assume <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose for a contradiction that <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a graph from <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> as an induced subgraph <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, there is a vertex <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)</annotation></semantics></math> is the long claw depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Since <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex of <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)=F\cap B_{G}(v_{F},4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cap B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)=F\cap B_{G}(v_{F},4/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the long claw is an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(v_{F},4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(v_{F},4/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Boland &amp; Lekkeikerker [], II). ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>. This completes the proof of the claim for <math id="S3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 4</span></span><span id="Thmclaim4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a><span id="Thmclaim4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> implies <a href="#S1.I2.i2" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(ii)</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Boland &amp; Lekkeikerker [], II). ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, it suffices to show that the <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-local cover <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> has no graph <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as an induced subgraph.
Suppose for a contradiction that <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a graph depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as an induced subgraph <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)\subseteq B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{F}(v_{F},4/2)\subseteq B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},4/2)</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#Thmclaim2" title="Claim 2. ‣ Proof of . ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Claim</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, assume that <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 5</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 5</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of the subgraph <math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},5/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},5/2)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves <math id="S3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-balls and <math id="S3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 5</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S3.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is also an induced subgraph of the induced subgraph <math id="S3.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(p_{r}(v_{F}),5/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(p_{r}(v_{F}),5/2)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts that <math id="S3.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is not an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, assume that <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of the subgraph <math id="S3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G_{r}}(v_{F},4/2)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves <math id="S3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r/2</annotation></semantics></math>-balls and <math id="S3.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S3.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is also an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(p_{r}(v_{F}),4/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(p_{r}(v_{F}),4/2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then only the blue vertices of <math id="S3.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> have distance <math id="S3.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r}(v_{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r}(v_{F})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is not the long claw, then it is a simple check that every potential edge of <math id="S3.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> between some pair of the blue vertices yields an induced cycle of length <math id="S3.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts that <math id="S3.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is in particular <math id="S3.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-chordal by <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>.
Thus assume that <math id="S3.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is the long claw.
Then, the addition of the potential edge of <math id="S3.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> between pairs of blue vertices in <a href="#S2.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a> gives one of the two graphs depicted in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which then is an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 5</span></span><span id="Thmclaim5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a><span id="Thmclaim5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> implies <a href="#S1.I2.i4" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iv)</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we note that it suffices to show that <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has none of the graphs depicted in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> as induced subgraphs.
Suppose <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> forbids each graph in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. By <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>, <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> forbids each graph in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Then <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a 3-acyclic circular-arc graph by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Cao, Grippo &amp; Safe [], Theorem 1). ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
As <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-locally chordal by <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3 ([], Theorem 1 and [], Theorem 1). ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, it follows that <math id="S3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact <math id="S3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2 ([], Theorem 5). ‣ 2.1. Locally chordal graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Therefore, it is sufficient to show that <math id="S3.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> forbids each graph in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> as an induced subgraph.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Every graph <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> except the one in <a href="#S3.F2.sf6" title="Figure 2f ‣ Figure 2 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2f</span></a> contains an induced cycle of length <math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus is not an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>.
It remains to show that the graph <math id="S3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> depicted in <a href="#S3.F2.sf6" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2f</span></a> is not an induced subgraph of <math id="S3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> contains an induced cycle of length 5, it follows from <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a> that <math id="S3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> excludes <math id="S3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> as an induced subgraph when <math id="S3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 5</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>, then the induced subgraph <math id="S3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the graph depicted in <a href="#S3.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3b</span></a>, which is a contradiction to <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmclaim6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_claim">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmclaim6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 6</span></span><span id="Thmclaim6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmclaim6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmclaim6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S1.I2.i4" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iv)</span></a><span id="Thmclaim6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> implies <a href="#S1.I2.i3" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Item</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(iii)</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Cao, Grippo &amp; Safe [], Theorem 1). ‣ 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 4&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 4&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, an <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic circular-arc graph <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains none of the graphs depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as induced subgraphs.
This completes the proof for <math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geqslant 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geqslant 5</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=4</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to observe that none of the graphs in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3. Proof of Theorem 1 ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is circular-arc.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof of <a href="#Thmmainresult1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1.1. Connections to local-global theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Characterization of 3-locally chordal graphs</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we revisit the case of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Conjecture 1.1 (Rzążewski 2024). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> where <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Conjecture 1.1 (Rzążewski 2024). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> is trivially wrong: every graph is <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-, <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-, and <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval, since the relevant balls are stars and stars are interval graphs, but there are graphs, e.g. the long claw depicted in <a href="#S2.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a>, which are not circular-arc.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Conjecture 1.1 (Rzążewski 2024). ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> fails for the same reason: the long claw is still <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval but not a circular-arc graph. However, every 3-acyclic circular-arc graph is 3-locally interval by definition.
One may characterize <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval graphs by forbidden induced subgraphs, as follows. A vertex <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in a graph <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an <em id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">apex vertex</em> if <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every other vertex in <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmainresult2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainresult">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmainresult2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmmainresult2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmainresult2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmainresult2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmainresult2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmmainresult2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite graph. Then the following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-locally interval.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is wheel-free (equivalently: </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-locally chordal) and contains none of the graphs depicted in </span><a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with an apex vertex added as an induced subgraph.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Every <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-locally interval graph is <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-locally chordal, as interval graphs are chordal. It is easy to check that each graph in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with an apex vertex added is not 3-locally interval.
Conversely, let <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-locally chordal graph such that <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains none of the graphs depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with an added apex vertex as induced subgraph.
Consider a vertex <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and the chordal <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>-ball <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\coloneqq B_{G}(v,3/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\coloneqq B_{G}(v,3/2)</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Boland &amp; Lekkeikerker [], II). ‣ 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, it suffices to show that <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> contains none of the graphs depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as an induced subgraph.
Suppose for a contradiction that <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> contains a graph from <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as an induced subgraph <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to all other vertices in <math id="S4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> but no vertex in the graphs depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.2. Interval graphs ‣ 2. Background ‣ Locally interval graphs are circular-arc graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is adjacent to all other vertices in that graph, <math id="S4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is not a vertex of <math id="S4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the subgraph induced on the vertex set of <math id="S4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> together with <math id="S4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is one of the induced subgraphs forbidden in <math id="S4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
